  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a memory management policy that discards the least recently used items when the cache reaches its capacity. This is widely used in databases, HTTP caches, and operating systems. Python\u2019s `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation because it maintains insertion order (and more importantly, **access order** when using `move_to_end`) and supports O(1) operations for `popitem`, `move_to_end`, and dictionary access.\n\nBelow is a complete, production-quality implementation with thorough documentation, followed by tests, a worked example, and a complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nA bounded LRU cache implemented using collections.OrderedDict.\n\nKey properties:\n- Fixed capacity at construction.\n- O(1) get and put operations.\n- Least recently used item is evicted on overflow.\n- Accessing an existing key moves it to the most-recently-used position.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded cache that evicts the least recently used entry when full.\n\n    Parameters:\n        capacity (int): Maximum number of items the cache can hold. Must be >= 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If capacity is less than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Capacity must be >= 1, got {capacity}\")\n        self._capacity = capacity\n        self._store: OrderedDict = OrderedDict()\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Public API\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with `key`.\n\n        - If the key exists, its value is returned AND the key is moved\n          to the most-recently-used position.\n        - If the key does not exist, None is returned.\n\n        Args:\n            key: Hashable key to look up.\n\n        Returns:\n            The associated value if found, else None.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self._store:\n            return None\n\n        # Move accessed key to the end (MRU position)\n        self._store.move_to_end(key)\n        return self._store[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value",
